    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Power_Transmission_Network\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    n_nodes = 5\n    nodes = list(range(n_nodes))\n    n_generators = 2\n\n    # Node coordinates (x, y) in km\n    node_coords = [(30, 66), (24, 59), (25, 14), (47, 10), (1, 22)]\n\n    # Node types\n    node_types = ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n\n    # Power demands and generator capacities (MW)\n    power_demands = [0.0, 0.0, 49.0, 43.0, 15.0]\n    generator_capacities = [188.0, 105.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n\n    # Wire physical properties (INDUCED PARAMETERS)\n    resistivity = 1.68e-08  # Ohm-meter\n    wire_area_mm2 = 500.0  # mm^2\n    wire_area_m2 = wire_area_mm2 * 1e-6  # Convert to m^2\n\n    # Operating parameters\n    operating_voltage = 220.0  # kV\n    line_capacity = 100.0  # MW\n    build_cost_per_km = 100000.0  # $/km\n    loss_cost_rate = 50.0  # $/MWh equivalent\n\n    # COMPUTE distance and resistance matrices (INDUCED COSTS)\n    # Using Ohm's Law: R = resistivity * length / area\n    def compute_distance_km(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    def compute_resistance(dist_km):\n        dist_m = dist_km * 1000  # Convert km to m\n        return resistivity * dist_m / wire_area_m2\n\n    distances = {}  # km\n    resistances = {}  # Ohm\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                dist = compute_distance_km(node_coords[i], node_coords[j])\n                distances[(i, j)] = dist\n                resistances[(i, j)] = compute_resistance(dist)\n\n    # Create edge list (undirected, i < j)\n    edges = [(i, j) for i in nodes for j in nodes if i < j]\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Build_i_j: Binary - whether to build line between nodes i and j\n    build = {}\n    for (i, j) in edges:\n        build[(i, j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Build_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Flow_i_j: Continuous - power flow from node i to j (MW)\n    flow = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                flow[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Helper function to get build variable (handles i > j case)\n    def get_build(i, j):\n        if i < j:\n            return build[(i, j)]\n        else:\n            return build[(j, i)]\n\n    # Objective: Minimize construction cost + operational transmission cost\n    # Cost factor: converts resistance to cost coefficient\n    # Cost = LossRate * (R / V^2) * P^2\n    # factor = LossRate / V^2\n    loss_factor = loss_cost_rate / (operating_voltage ** 2)\n\n    # Construction costs (based on distance)\n    obj = quicksum(distances[(i, j)] * build_cost_per_km * build[(i, j)] for (i, j) in edges)\n\n    # Operational costs: Quadratic (I^2*R losses)\n    # Note: Gurobi supports quadratic objectives directly\n    obj += quicksum(\n        resistances[(i, j)] * loss_factor * flow[(i, j)] * flow[(i, j)]\n        for i in nodes for j in nodes if i != j\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Flow conservation constraints\n    for node in nodes:\n        inflow = quicksum(flow[(other, node)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        outflow = quicksum(flow[(node, other)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        \n        if node_types[node] == 'generator':\n            # Generator: net outflow (generation) <= capacity\n            # Rewritten: inflow - outflow >= -capacity\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= -generator_capacities[node],\n                name=f\"Gen_Cap_{node}\"\n            )\n        else:\n            # Consumer: net inflow >= demand\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= power_demands[node],\n                name=f\"Demand_{node}\"\n            )\n\n    # Capacity constraints: Flow <= line_capacity * Build\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    flow[(i, j)] <= line_capacity * get_build(i, j),\n                    name=f\"Cap_{i}_{j}\"\n                )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Planning brief \u2013 cost\u2011optimal five\u2011node transmission network using copper conductors\n\nOur utility needs to connect two generator sites and three consumer load points into a single transmission network and do so at the lowest total cost. The five locations are given in Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (30, 66)\n  - N1 (generator): (24, 59)\n  - N2 (consumer): (25, 14)\n  - N3 (consumer): (47, 10)\n  - N4 (consumer): (1, 22), with the role of each site identified in ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']. Consumer demand levels are specified in Consumer demands: N2: 49.0 MW, N3: 43.0 MW, N4: 15.0 MW, and the two generators have finite output capability summarized in Generator capacities: N0: 188.0 MW, N1: 105.0 MW. Every consumer must receive at least its stated requirement; delivering more than the minimum is acceptable if it helps the overall plan. For planning purposes, we will treat power flow as conserved at each node\u2014thermal losses are accounted for financially and are not subtracted from these balances.\n\nWe can build transmission lines between any pair of sites; there are no pre\u2011defined corridors in this study. Any line we choose to construct can carry power in either direction, up to a symmetric rating of 100.0 MW. Construction cost is distance\u2011based at $100,000/km for each kilometer of line installed.\n\nAll lines will use copper conductors. The wire material specification is provided in 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper), and the conductor size is given by 500.0 mm\u00b2. Line resistance increases with length and decreases with cross\u2011section, so longer spans and smaller conductors incur higher heating. These resistive losses follow a square law: the heating rises with the square of the current (I\u00b2R). Because current equals power divided by voltage, operating at 220.0 kV means the heating on a line scales with its resistance times the square of (power/voltage). In practical terms, if we double the power flow on a given line, the associated heating cost becomes roughly four times larger. We monetize this heating at $50.0/MWh; equivalently, the loss cost on a line is $50.0/MWh multiplied by its resistance and by (power/220.0 kV) squared. Higher voltage directly reduces current for a given power, shrinking heating costs with a 1/V\u00b2 effect.\n\nOur goal is to decide which lines to build and how much power to route over each built line so that all consumer demands are met within generator limits while minimizing the sum of construction expenditures and the quadratic \u201cheating costs\u201d of resistive losses. We will not rely on simple linear proxies for losses or on merely summing flows; instead, the plan must reflect the physical reality that pushing more power through a line sharply increases losses under the square law. The resulting network design should strike the right balance between building longer paths that reduce current and losses versus shorter routes that might seem cheaper to build but become disproportionately expensive to operate as flows increase.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Planning brief \u2013 cost\u2011optimal five\u2011node transmission network using copper conductors\n\nOur utility needs to connect two generator sites and three consumer load points into a single transmission network and do so at the lowest total cost. The five locations are given in {NODE_COORDINATES}, with the role of each site identified in {NODE_TYPES}. Consumer demand levels are specified in {POWER_DEMANDS}, and the two generators have finite output capability summarized in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}. Every consumer must receive at least its stated requirement; delivering more than the minimum is acceptable if it helps the overall plan. For planning purposes, we will treat power flow as conserved at each node\u2014thermal losses are accounted for financially and are not subtracted from these balances.\n\nWe can build transmission lines between any pair of sites; there are no pre\u2011defined corridors in this study. Any line we choose to construct can carry power in either direction, up to a symmetric rating of {LINE_CAPACITY}. Construction cost is distance\u2011based at {BUILD_COST_PER_KM} for each kilometer of line installed.\n\nAll lines will use copper conductors. The wire material specification is provided in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, and the conductor size is given by {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}. Line resistance increases with length and decreases with cross\u2011section, so longer spans and smaller conductors incur higher heating. These resistive losses follow a square law: the heating rises with the square of the current (I\u00b2R). Because current equals power divided by voltage, operating at {OPERATING_VOLTAGE} means the heating on a line scales with its resistance times the square of (power/voltage). In practical terms, if we double the power flow on a given line, the associated heating cost becomes roughly four times larger. We monetize this heating at {LOSS_COST_RATE}; equivalently, the loss cost on a line is {LOSS_COST_RATE} multiplied by its resistance and by (power/{OPERATING_VOLTAGE}) squared. Higher voltage directly reduces current for a given power, shrinking heating costs with a 1/V\u00b2 effect.\n\nOur goal is to decide which lines to build and how much power to route over each built line so that all consumer demands are met within generator limits while minimizing the sum of construction expenditures and the quadratic \u201cheating costs\u201d of resistive losses. We will not rely on simple linear proxies for losses or on merely summing flows; instead, the plan must reflect the physical reality that pushing more power through a line sharply increases losses under the square law. The resulting network design should strike the right balance between building longer paths that reduce current and losses versus shorter routes that might seem cheaper to build but become disproportionately expensive to operate as flows increase."
}